1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,491
Aarav's torch runs on a cell.

2
00:00:02,491 --> 00:00:05,264
His table fan runs from the wall socket.

3
00:00:05,614 --> 00:00:07,341
Both use electric current.

4
00:00:07,341 --> 00:00:10,042
So why can't the fan just run on a cell?

5
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:11,893
Start with the cell.

6
00:00:11,893 --> 00:00:14,040
It gives direct current, or DC.

7
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:16,879
Direct current flows
in one direction only.

8
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:21,458
Out of the plus terminal, round the
circuit, and back into the minus terminal.

9
00:00:21,808 --> 00:00:25,016
Draw current against time,
and you get a flat line.

10
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:27,647
Same size, same direction, all the time.

11
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:32,998
DC can also rise and fall in size,
like the output of a rectifier, which

12
00:00:32,998 --> 00:00:34,480
we will meet later.

13
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,384
But it never turns around.

14
00:00:37,034 --> 00:00:40,591
Cells, batteries and solar
cells all give direct current.

15
00:00:41,241 --> 00:00:42,681
Now the wall socket.

16
00:00:42,681 --> 00:00:45,214
It gives alternating current, or AC.

17
00:00:45,564 --> 00:00:48,499
Alternating current keeps
changing its direction.

18
00:00:48,499 --> 00:00:52,556
It rises to a peak, falls to
zero, then flows the other way.

19
00:00:52,906 --> 00:00:57,143
Draw it against time, and you get
a smooth wave called a sine wave.

20
00:00:57,493 --> 00:01:00,426
Power stations make it with AC generators.

21
00:01:00,426 --> 00:01:05,661
A coil turns inside a magnetic field,
and every half turn, the push reverses.

22
00:01:06,311 --> 00:01:08,448
Let's see both on the breadboard.

23
00:01:08,448 --> 00:01:11,175
One resistor, and a small indicator lamp.

24
00:01:11,525 --> 00:01:13,089
First, connect a cell.

25
00:01:13,089 --> 00:01:16,088
The electrons march one
way, at a steady speed.

26
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:20,772
The scope draws a flat line, and the
current reads plus zero point two

27
00:01:20,772 --> 00:01:22,404
amperes, all the time.

28
00:01:23,054 --> 00:01:24,847
Now switch to an AC source.

29
00:01:24,847 --> 00:01:27,577
We have slowed it right
down so you can watch.

30
00:01:27,927 --> 00:01:32,238
The electrons move forward, slow
down, stop, and then move backward.

31
00:01:32,588 --> 00:01:34,547
The scope draws a sine wave.

32
00:01:34,547 --> 00:01:38,923
The reading swings from plus to
minus, and the lamp dims each time the

33
00:01:38,923 --> 00:01:40,317
current passes zero.

34
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:44,062
In AC, the electrons do not
travel round the circuit.

35
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:46,971
They rock back and forth,
passing energy along.

36
00:01:47,621 --> 00:01:49,161
DC flows one way.

37
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:50,965
AC keeps turning back.

38
00:01:51,765 --> 00:01:53,956
So how fast does AC turn back?

39
00:01:53,956 --> 00:01:55,387
Two numbers tell us.

40
00:01:55,737 --> 00:01:59,289
One full forward and backward
swing is called a cycle.

41
00:01:59,289 --> 00:02:03,002
The time for one cycle is
the period, T, in seconds.

42
00:02:03,702 --> 00:02:07,478
The number of cycles in one
second is the frequency, f.

43
00:02:07,478 --> 00:02:09,136
Its unit is the hertz.

44
00:02:09,136 --> 00:02:11,516
One hertz is one cycle per second.

45
00:02:12,216 --> 00:02:13,328
They are linked.

46
00:02:13,328 --> 00:02:14,728
f equals one over T.

47
00:02:15,428 --> 00:02:18,978
And in every cycle, the current
changes direction twice.

48
00:02:19,628 --> 00:02:20,731
Worked example.

49
00:02:20,731 --> 00:02:25,447
In India, the mains supply is about
two hundred and thirty volts, at fifty

50
00:02:25,447 --> 00:02:26,347
hertz.

51
00:02:26,397 --> 00:02:28,549
The period is one over fifty.

52
00:02:28,549 --> 00:02:32,675
That is zero point zero two
seconds, or twenty milliseconds.

53
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:36,131
Two reversals per cycle,
times fifty cycles.

54
00:02:36,131 --> 00:02:40,366
So the current changes direction
one hundred times every second.

55
00:02:41,016 --> 00:02:42,763
So why do homes get AC?

56
00:02:43,113 --> 00:02:48,229
AC voltage can be raised or lowered easily
with a transformer, and a transformer

57
00:02:48,229 --> 00:02:49,784
works only with AC.

58
00:02:50,134 --> 00:02:55,119
Power is sent over long distances at
very high voltage, because that wastes

59
00:02:55,119 --> 00:02:57,313
far less energy in the wires.

60
00:02:57,663 --> 00:03:00,278
But phones and laptops need steady DC.

61
00:03:00,278 --> 00:03:02,902
So their chargers change AC into DC.

62
00:03:02,902 --> 00:03:03,907
And the fan?

63
00:03:03,907 --> 00:03:07,947
It is built to run on AC, at
two hundred and thirty volts.

64
00:03:07,947 --> 00:03:09,655
A cell cannot give that.

65
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:11,770
Now, the exam trap.

66
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:16,277
Many students think fifty hertz means
the current reverses fifty times

67
00:03:16,277 --> 00:03:17,177
a second.

68
00:03:17,437 --> 00:03:19,735
But each cycle has two reversals.

69
00:03:19,735 --> 00:03:23,385
So fifty hertz means one
hundred reversals every second.

70
00:03:24,035 --> 00:03:25,641
Where is this asked?

71
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:27,248
In PUC board papers.

72
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:32,627
Differences between AC and DC, and
short problems on frequency and period.

73
00:03:33,277 --> 00:03:34,177
Try this.

74
00:03:34,077 --> 00:03:37,248
An AC source has a period
of four milliseconds.

75
00:03:37,248 --> 00:03:41,209
How many times does its current
change direction in one second?

76
00:03:46,609 --> 00:03:47,509
C.

77
00:03:47,118 --> 00:03:48,832
Five hundred times.

78
00:03:49,182 --> 00:03:50,853
First, the frequency.

79
00:03:50,853 --> 00:03:55,432
f equals one over T, one over
zero point zero zero four seconds.

80
00:03:55,432 --> 00:03:58,044
That is two hundred and fifty hertz.

81
00:03:58,394 --> 00:04:00,316
Each cycle has two reversals.

82
00:04:00,316 --> 00:04:03,383
Two times two hundred and
fifty is five hundred.

83
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:05,554
Screenshot this card.

84
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:11,441
Next, Ohm's law and electrical resistance.

85
00:04:11,441 --> 00:04:16,086
Why does the same push give a big
current in one wire, and a tiny one in

86
00:04:16,086 --> 00:04:16,986
another?
